WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.040
In the history of art, there are great&nbsp;
artists... and then there is Caravaggio.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.360
In his time, no great painter&nbsp;
began a work with a brush. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:15.120
they drew. They made sketches and studies of&nbsp;
each figure, then prepared a full-size drawing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.240
and only then transferred it onto the canvas.&nbsp;
It was the method the great masters of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.280
Renaissance had followed, and for many,&nbsp;
it was the only correct way to paint.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.400
Caravaggio, however, did not paint this way.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:34.800
And this painting is a perfect example of that.&nbsp;
In it, we see Christ bound to a column, surrounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.160
by two men preparing to scourge him. There is&nbsp;
no landscape around them, and no architecture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.680
that would allow us to recognize where they are.&nbsp;
We only see bodies emerging from the darkness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.200
lit by a light falling from above, as if someone&nbsp;
had opened a window in the middle of the night.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.560
But the most impressive&nbsp;
thing is the body of Christ.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.560
Every muscle seems to rise out of&nbsp;
the shadow with absolute precision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.200
as if we were looking at a real&nbsp;
person just a few inches away from us.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.000
And the most incredible part is that,&nbsp;
most likely, Caravaggio did not make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.520
a single preparatory drawing to create it. He&nbsp;
would place his models in front of the easel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.000
light them with a single light source,&nbsp;
and paint directly onto the canvas.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.840
A way of working so radical that many tried to&nbsp;
imitate it, but none ever managed to equal it.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.600
This work is called Christ at&nbsp;
the Column, also known as The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:34.480
Flagellation of Christ. It was painted&nbsp;
by Caravaggio between 1606 and 1607,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.040
and today it is held at the Museum&nbsp;
of Fine Arts in Rouen, France.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:45.680
By that time, Caravaggio's life had taken a&nbsp;
dramatic turn. In 1606 he killed a man in Rome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.880
was sentenced to death, and&nbsp;
had to escape to Naples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:52.160
where the powerful Colonna&nbsp;
family gave him protection.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.960
And even while living as a fugitive,&nbsp;
Caravaggio kept painting. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.600
some of his most intense works were born&nbsp;
precisely during this stage of his life.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.880
We must keep in mind that in sixteenth-century&nbsp;
Italy, drawing was everything.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.520
Artists called it disegno, and for them&nbsp;
it did not simply mean tracing lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.000
on paper. It meant thinking through&nbsp;
the work. Conceiving it in the mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.320
perfecting it, and idealizing it&nbsp;
before ever touching the canvas.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.600
The process was almost always the same. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.440
sketches were made. Then those&nbsp;
sketches were turned into a final,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.800
full-size drawing called a cartoon. And that&nbsp;
cartoon was transferred onto the surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.640
where the work would finally be painted. But&nbsp;
Caravaggio broke completely with that tradition.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:44.000
All of his early biographers agree on the same&nbsp;
thing: he was known for not making preliminary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.520
drawings, and he was capable of painting directly&nbsp;
onto the canvas with his models posing in front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:55.360
of him. And for his time, that was one of the&nbsp;
greatest innovations of his way of working.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.960
In fact, to this day, practically none of his&nbsp;
drawings survive. What experts have found are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.000
small incisions in the preparatory layer of&nbsp;
his canvases, but these were simply marks he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.240
made with the end of his brush handle to place his&nbsp;
figures, and from there, he would begin to paint.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.520
For many artists of his time, this was&nbsp;
almost a lack of respect for the craft.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.560
When the painter Federico Zuccari visited&nbsp;
the Contarelli Chapel in Rome, where some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:28.320
Caravaggio's first great works were displayed,&nbsp;
he said he saw nothing in them but the ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:34.000
of Giorgione. And that was not a compliment.&nbsp;
It was a reference to the old dispute between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.920
those who defended drawing and those who painted&nbsp;
directly with color, working from the live model.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:44.080
Even the physician and collector Giulio&nbsp;
Mancini pointed out the great problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:49.520
with this method. According to him, painting&nbsp;
from life worked well for a single figure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:53.280
but not for a large narrative scene,&nbsp;
because it was impossible to put a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.360
crowd of people in a room and ask&nbsp;
them to stand still to be copied.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.840
And this is where this painting becomes&nbsp;
fascinating. Because when we look at it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:07.120
we can see exactly how Caravaggio managed&nbsp;
to solve this problem in a masterful way.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:12.960
For this work, Caravaggio completely flattened&nbsp;
the space and reduced the figures to a minimum.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.720
We could think that this simplicity&nbsp;
is not just an aesthetic decision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:22.000
but also a consequence of his method. If you&nbsp;
are going to paint everything from real people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:28.000
posing in front of you, you need few figures,&nbsp;
very close together, and under the same light.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.360
And that is exactly what we see. A horizontal&nbsp;
composition, with a very tight frame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.840
and three-quarter-length figures that&nbsp;
seem to be just a few inches away from us.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.800
Then there is the light. It falls from above,&nbsp;
as if it were coming through a small window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:48.000
in the middle of a dark room. And as&nbsp;
historian Andrew Graham-Dixon explains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.640
Caravaggio used it to direct our gaze exactly&nbsp;
where he wanted: the face and torso of Christ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:58.400
the faces of the executioners,&nbsp;
and the hand holding the whip.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.560
The secret of Caravaggio's technique,&nbsp;
and something that set him apart from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.600
the rest of the artists of his time, is that&nbsp;
instead of constructing the light in a drawing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.600
he physically placed it on his&nbsp;
models and painted what he saw.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:17.840
That is why his shadows do not feel invented,&nbsp;
but like real shadows cast by real bodies.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.480
Finally, there is another detail that&nbsp;
makes his method even more fascinating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:28.000
According to Bellori, one of his early&nbsp;
biographers, Caravaggio left the preparatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.480
layer of the canvas visible in some&nbsp;
areas to use it as a middle tone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:37.760
especially in the skin and hair. This means&nbsp;
he did not even cover the entire surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:42.480
but painted only what was necessary&nbsp;
and let the light do the rest.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:48.240
Something worth keeping in mind is that his way of&nbsp;
painting was so revolutionary that it gave rise to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.920
an entire generation of followers known as the&nbsp;
Caravaggisti. And some of them copied his style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:59.200
with such precision that this very painting ended&nbsp;
up being mistaken for the work of one of them.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.800
When the museum in Rouen bought it in 1955,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.640
it did so believing it was a work by Mattia&nbsp;
Preti, a Baroque painter who worked in Naples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.520
and was deeply influenced by Caravaggio.&nbsp;
And it paid around 225,000 francs for it.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:17.600
Until, in 1959, the historian Roberto&nbsp;
Longhi, the greatest Caravaggio expert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.400
of the twentieth century, stood in front&nbsp;
of it. And according to Michel Laclotte,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:26.160
who was with him and who years later&nbsp;
would become director of the Louvre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:31.040
Longhi began moving in front of the&nbsp;
canvas in a kind of silent dance.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:36.880
He immediately recognized that the painting had&nbsp;
been made by Caravaggio, and from that moment on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.920
a painting that belonged to an ordinary&nbsp;
provincial museum became a masterpiece.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:45.440
The lesson in all of this is that,&nbsp;
throughout the history of art,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:51.920
many tried to paint like Caravaggio. They copied&nbsp;
his light, his darkness, and his realism. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:57.200
what made Caravaggio unique was not only&nbsp;
what he painted, but how he painted it.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:02.720
That is why we could say that, although there&nbsp;
have been many Caravaggisti over the centuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:07.120
there will surely never be another Caravaggio.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.600
Thank you for watching this video until the end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

